1942年的记录。这是1914年秋天的事，这是对俄军占领当时属于加利西亚的登比察镇的目击记述。多米尼克，我们讨论了很多关于军队、战斗以及所有这些事情，旗帜飘扬、长笛吹奏、蒂罗尔帽子上的羽毛飘动。但我们没有讨论的是，这一切对普通平民意味着什么。我猜没有什么好事。绝对没有好事。是的，这其实是一个讨论战争目标以及人们对战争诉求的机会。因此，俄军向西挺进，在我看来，他们带有所有主要参赞国中最激进、最具变革性的议程。他们一进入加利西亚，新任被占领土总督格奥尔吉·博布林斯基伯爵就立刻明确了这一点。

65
00:33:12.000 --> 00:33:48.000
When he got into Leviv, he gathered all the local bigwigs and the bishops, and he made a speech. And he said, the lands of Eastern Galicia are an eternal part of a single great Russia. In these lands, the true population was always Russian. Thus, the administration of this land should be based on Russian principles. We'll introduce the Russian language, Russian law, and the Russian system. Definite resonances, yeah. Now, this was very bad news for the Poles, the people who dominated the political and economic life of the towns of Galicia. It was extremely bad news for the Ukrainians, because they were told,
他一进入利沃夫，就召集了当地所有名流和主教，并发表了一讲。他说，东加利西亚的土地是一个伟大的单一俄罗斯永恒的一部分。在这些土地上，真正的居民一直是俄罗斯人。因此，这片土地的行政管理应当以俄罗斯原则为基础。我们将引入俄语、俄罗斯法律和俄罗斯制度。确实有似曾相识的感觉，是的。现在，这对波兰人来说是个非常坏的消息，波兰人主导了加利西亚城镇的政治和经济生活。这对乌克兰人来说是极其糟糕的消息，因为他们被告知：

66
00:33:48.000 --> 00:34:18.000
You don't exist. Your church, your Greek Catholic church, your Ukrainian language, your separate Ukrainian, or as people would have said then, Ruthenian identity, all of these things, which interestingly had been not just tolerated, but often encouraged by the Austro-Hungarians as a counterweight to the polls. All of these things will now be erased. From now on, you are Russian, whether you like it or not. Now, if it's bad for them, it is unbelievably bad for the third ethnic group in Galicia, which are the Jews.
你们不存在。你们的教堂，你们的希腊天主教教堂，你们的乌克兰语，你们独立的乌克兰身份（或者当时人们所说的鲁塞尼亚身份），所有这些不仅被奥匈帝国容忍、甚至经常被其鼓励作为对抗波兰人的制衡力量的事物。所有这些现在都将被抹去。从现在开始，不管你愿意不愿意，你都是俄罗斯人。现在，如果这对他们来说很糟糕，那么对于加利西亚的第三大族群——犹太人来说，简直是糟透了。

67
00:34:18.000 --> 00:34:41.000
Austria, Hungary was not exactly a paradise for Jews. Because we talked before when we did the rise of Nazism about the influence of Vienna's mayor, Karl Nuger, on Hitler, this kind of populist, anti-Semitic mayor. But isn't part of that because there is a resentment of Jews in Vienna, say, who have done incredibly well? Yes. So Freud, Marla. Massive cultural figures.
奥匈帝国对犹太人来说并非真正的人间天堂。因为我们之前在讨论纳粹主义崛起时谈到过维也纳市长卡尔·鲁格对希特勒的影响，这种民粹主义的、反犹太主义的市长。但部分原因难道不是因为维也纳人对犹太人取得巨大成功心存怨恨吗？是的。所以弗洛伊德、马勒。巨大的文化人物。

68
00:34:41.000 --> 00:35:21.000
Jews are very prominent in the empire's high culture. There's clearly a place for them in a multinational empire. like the Habsburg Empire. And they're very loyal to the dynasty. So Franz Joseph had lifted a lot of the existing punitive laws against Jews. Franz Joseph had made a pilgrimage, effectively to Jerusalem. He made a point of meeting Jewish leaders. So there is, you know, there's clearly, Austria, Hungary is a relatively warm and welcoming home if you're an Eastern European Jew. The Russian Empire takes a very different approach to religious diversity. Russia is comfortably the most anti-Semitic country in Europe at this point.
犹太人在帝国的上层文化中非常显眼。在一个多民族帝国（如哈布斯堡帝国）中，显然有他们的一席之地。而且他们对王朝非常忠诚。因此，弗兰茨·约瑟夫取消了许多针对犹太人的现有惩罚性法律。弗兰茨·约瑟夫甚至去耶路撒冷朝圣。他特意去会见了犹太领袖。所以你知道，奥匈帝国对于东欧犹太人来说，显然是一个相对温暖和温馨的家园。俄罗斯帝国对待宗教多样性的态度则截然不同。此时的俄罗斯无疑是欧洲最反犹太主义的国家。

69
00:35:21.000 --> 00:36:07.000
It's here in 1903 and it's no accident that conspiracy theorists faked the protocols of the elders of Zion. I think the interesting thing about Russian anti-Semitism is that it's obviously partly rooted in history, but it's also a very modern phenomenon. And a lot of historians say the reason it becomes so intense is that Russia is changing so much in the late 19th century, economic change, industrialization, urbanization, a lot of people who are losing. out and they, the Jews become scapegoats. So from the 1880s in Russia, the harshest anti-Semitic laws in Europe. And then a wave upon wave of pogroms. So Kiev in 1881, Kishenev very famously in 1903, Adessa in 1905, hundreds of people killed. And this is why there are
正是在1903年的这里，阴谋论者伪造《锡安长老会纪要》绝非偶然。我认为关于俄罗斯反犹太主义有趣的一点是，它显然部分植根于历史，但它也是一个非常现代的现象。许多历史学家表示，它变得如此强烈的原因是俄罗斯在19世纪后期发生了巨大变化，经济变革、工业化、城市化，许多人破产了，而犹太人成了替罪羊。因此，从19世纪80年代开始，俄罗斯出台了欧洲最严厉的反犹太法律。随之而来的是一波又一波的排犹大暴动。因此，1881年的基辅、1903年极具名气的基什尼奥夫、1905年的敖德萨，数百人被杀。这就是为什么会出现

70
00:36:07.000 --> 00:36:39.000
kind of waves of emigration, aren't they coming to Britain and then even more to the United States. Exactly. So if you're, you know, a Jewish village. or a townsman or whatever in 1914, in Galicia, and you hear the Russians are coming, you are terrified. And people have a particular dread of the Cossacks. You open this half by doing a reading about the Cossacks, because the Cossacks see themselves as the defenders of Russian imperialism and Russian orthodoxy, and they have a blood-soaked reputation. They have been the shock troops in a lot of these pogroms.
一波又一波的移民潮，不是吗？他们来到英国，然后更多地去往美国。没错。所以如果你在1914年的加利西亚是一个犹太村庄的居民或城镇居民，当你听说俄军要来时，你会吓坏的。人们对哥萨克人有一种特殊的恐惧。你这集开头讲了一段关于哥萨克人的内容，因为哥萨克人将自己视为俄罗斯帝国主义和俄罗斯正统信仰的捍卫者，他们有着血迹斑斑的名声。他们一直是许多次排犹大暴动中的突击队。

71
00:36:39.000 --> 00:37:16.000
And as we've heard, they completely live up to this horrendous billing. As soon as they cross the frontier, there were stories about them attacking. Jewish villages, looting shops, killing people, raping people, all of this. And the very worst pogrom was in Leviv at the end of September. It's a common story in the First World War that there'll be a rumor goes around, oh, our troops are being attacked by partisans or whatever. So this is what happened in Leviv, and there the Cossacks were given permission. Their commander said, okay, the gloves are off, you can go and punish people, do what you like. And they ran amok, they slaughtered almost 50 people, and they rounded up 300 people for
正如我们所听到的那样，他们完全符合这种可怕的形象。他们一过边境，就不断传出他们袭击犹太村庄、洗劫店铺、杀害平民、强奸妇女等各种故事。最严重的大暴动发生在9月底的利沃夫。第一次世界大战中有一个常见的故事，就是会流传着这样的谣言，哦，我们的部队正遭到游击队或诸如此类的袭击。利沃夫发生的就是这种情况，当时哥萨克人得到了授权。他们的指挥官说，好了，不用客气了，你们可以去惩罚他们，随心所欲吧。于是他们大开杀戒，屠杀了近50人，并搜捕了300人准备

72
00:37:16.000 --> 00:38:26.000
deportation to the east. It's not yet genocidal. It's nothing, I think that would be a massive overstatement. However, the direction of travel, I would say, is pretty ominous. A lot of the Russian commanders are obsessively anti-Semitic. The chief of staff, who's a guy called General Yanushkiewicz, is even by Russian standards, he's a pathological anti-Semite. And so there's no effort made to moderate the violence at all. In fact, officers will have Jewish people black, They'll blackmail them. They'll loot their shops and houses. They have them whipped in the streets, hanged and so on. And then in the spring of 1915, the Russians begin mass deportations, huge deportations of Jews to camps in the East. So this is the shadow that is hanging. This is the threat that awaits the people of Shemichel in the turn of 1914 into 1915. Of course, the Russians haven't taken the city yet. They have learned their lesson from the first seats. They're not going to do this kind of frontal attack, they're going to starve the defenders out. Now, the guy commanding the fortress, this man called General Kusmanek, who is a policeman's son from
将其流放到东方。这还不是灭绝性的。我认为这绝非事实，这样说未免太夸张了。然而，大体趋势可以说是相当不祥的。许多俄军指挥官都有着偏执的反犹太主义倾向。参谋长是一个名叫亚努什凯维奇将军的人，甚至按俄罗斯的标准来看，他也是一个病态的反犹太主义者。因此，根本没有做任何努力来缓和暴力。事实上，军官们会勒索犹太人。他们会洗劫犹太人的店铺和房屋。他们让犹太人在街上挨鞭子、被绞死等等。然后在1915年春天，俄军开始大规模驱逐犹太人，将他们大量驱逐到东方的集中营。这就是悬在头顶的阴影。这就是1914转入1915年之际，普热梅希尔（Shemichel）人民所面临的威胁。当然，俄军还没有攻占这座城市。他们从第一次围攻中吸取了教训。他们不会进行这种正面进攻，而是要将守军围困至死。现在，指挥这座要塞的人，这个名叫库斯马内克将军的人，是一个来自

73
00:38:26.000 --> 00:39:03.000
Transylvania. He was an archive man, Tom. He was a desk officer who had worked in the Austrian War Archive. So we have laughed at the idea of academics and lawyers manning the defences, but actually they're coming out of this tremendously well, aren't they? It was a cheap shot, but irresistible, but maybe cheap nonetheless. So this bloke does very well. He says, you know, we're going to, he runs the city very smoothly under the circumstances. He sets up suit kitchens for people who haven't got any food. He gets nuns to cook hundreds of meals for them a day. However, he's really up against it because the conditions are, it's your classic kind of siege conditions.
特兰西瓦尼亚的警察的儿子。汤姆，他是个档案工作者。他是一个曾在奥地利战争档案馆工作过的内勤军官。所以我们曾嘲笑由学者和律师来把守防线这种想法，但实际上他们干得极其出色，不是吗？这是一个便宜的讽刺，但又让人无法抗拒，不过或许确实有些廉价。所以这家伙干得很棒。他说，你知道的，我们要，在那种情况下，他把这座城市管理得井井有条。他为没有食物的人设立了汤厨房。他让修女们每天为他们烹饪数千顿饭。然而，他确实面临巨大困境，因为环境就是典型的围城环境。

74
00:39:03.000 --> 00:39:35.000
So piles of unburied excrement in the streets, disease, rampant, drains overflowing, nothing to eat, all of this kind of thing. You know, the stuff you would see in siege is going back to antiquity. but there are some new elements. So at the beginning of December, the 1st of December, people look up, they hear something in the sky above the city, and they see a plane, and people come out actually to stare at it, watch it, and then they see these little gray balls falling out of the plane, and somebody suddenly realizes what they are and shouts.
街上堆积着未掩埋的排泄物，疾病横行，下水道溢出，没有东西吃，诸如此类的事情。你知道的，你在古代围城战中见到的那些东西。但也有一些新元素。因此，在12月初，即12月1日，人们抬起头，听到城市上空有什么声音，他们看到了一架飞机，人们甚至纷纷走出来盯着它看，观察它，然后他们看到一个小小的灰色球体从飞机上落下来，有人突然意识到那是什么并大喊。

75
00:39:35.000 --> 00:39:55.000
They're bombs, and there are people running in panic and whatnot. This is one of the first instances in history of the aerial bombing of civilians. So in all, the Russians launched 300 bombing raids on Shemishel. They were hoping, I think, to hit the bridges. But they don't, do they? No, they miss. I mean, just throwing a ball out of a plane. I mean, it's never going to land where you think it's going to land.
那是炸弹，人们开始惊慌失措地奔跑等。这是历史上对平民进行空中轰炸的最早实例之一。因此，俄军总共对普热梅希尔发动了300次轰炸。我想，他们是希望击中桥梁的。但他们没有，对吧？不，他们打偏了。我是说，从飞机上扔下一个球，它永远不可能落到你以为它会落到的地方。

76
00:39:55.000 --> 00:40:15.000
However, you know, it's not comedy. A dozen people were killed. Two dozen people were injured. There's a story, I think it's an Alexander Watson's account, of a girl, she had half her head cleaved off by shrapnel and had to be rushed twitching to hospital. But the bombings, as is always in history, The bombings terrify and are poor people, but they don't sap their will to resist because
然而，这不是喜剧。十几人丧生。二十几人受伤。我记得在亚历山大·沃森的记述中有一个故事，一个女孩的一半头颅被弹片削掉，不得不抽搐着被紧急送往医院。但轰炸正如历史上常有的一样，轰炸恐吓并折磨了可怜的人民，但并没有动摇他们抵抗的意志，因为

77
00:40:15.000 --> 00:40:57.000
people think, well, the Russians are barbarians. They're monsters to do this. We can never give into them. So Christmas comes and the city is still under siege. In Eastern Europe, the big day is Christmas Eve. The defenders tried to put up trees and they were given special food, but it's all a bit sad. Alexander Watson quotes this guy, Lieutenant Staniswof Gajach. And he says in his diary, I couldn't stop thinking about my wife and children. The day was was really difficult, even getting through it was so hard. He gathers his kind of platoon or whatever to celebrate the Holy Day and everybody starts crying. And then he had dinner with his fellow officers and they tried to light candles and sink carols. But again, they all start crying.
人们认为，俄军是野蛮人。做这种事简直是怪物。我们绝不能向他们屈服。圣诞节到了，这座城市依然处于围困之中。在东欧，最重要的日子是平安夜。守军试图竖起圣诞树，还分到了特殊的食物，但这一切都有点悲凉。亚历山大·沃森引用了这位名叫斯坦尼斯瓦夫·加亚奇中尉的话。他在日记中写道，我无法停止想念我的妻子和孩子。这一天真的很难熬，甚至熬过去都那么艰难。他召集了他的排或类似编制的人来庆祝这个神圣的节日，所有人都开始哭泣。然后他和同僚军官共进晚餐，他们试图点燃蜡烛并唱颂歌。但同样，他们都开始哭泣。

78
00:40:57.000 --> 00:41:38.000
Mostovsky, Stumpf, the captain, all quietly cried. And this is the one moment in the story actually where the Russians, belying their reputation, behaved, I think, very well. Because When Austrian patrols went out of the city that day, they found little parcels of bread and sausages and sugar in no man's land with cards wishing them a happy Christmas. Oh, that is nice. And Alexander Watson quotes a lovely example. Gallant nights, at so great a holiday as Christmas Eve, we wish you and your family is the best, and wish you return healthy to your nearest and dearest. We shall not disturb you on Christmas Day as you eat your supper and talk of your loved ones. As a mark of our fraternal greetings, we break this holy wafer with you.
莫斯托夫斯基、施通普夫船长，所有人都默默地哭了。而这其实是故事中唯一一个俄罗斯人摒弃其名声、表现得非常好的时刻。因为当奥地利巡逻队那天走出城市时，他们发现在无人区里放着一小包面包、香肠和糖，旁边还有祝他们圣诞快乐的卡片。哦，那太好了。亚历山大·沃森引用了一个极好的例子。勇敢的骑士们，在圣诞夜这样一个伟大的节日里，我们祝愿您和您的家人一切都好，祝您健康地回到亲人身边。在您吃晚餐和谈论挚爱的时候，我们在圣诞节不会打扰您。作为我们兄弟般问候的标志，我们与您分享这神圣的圣饼。

79
00:41:38.000 --> 00:42:01.000
your comrades outside the Siedliska forts. I mean, that's quite sweet in a way, isn't it? Yeah, so another Christmas truce on the Eastern Front as well as one on the Western Front. Yeah, and as on the Western Front, as soon as it's over, things get very dark indeed. Once we're into January, they've run out of food inside the fort. They basically start eating all their horses. But they haven't got on to the rats yet. They haven't got onto the rats.
你们在谢利利斯卡（Siedliska）堡垒外的战友。从某种意义上说，这挺温馨的，不是吗？是的，东线也出现了另一次圣诞休战，西线也是如此。是的，就像在西线一样，一旦结束，情况就立刻变得非常黑暗。一进入一月份，堡垒内部的食物就耗尽了。他们基本上开始吃所有的马匹。但他们还没沦落到吃老鼠的地步。他们还没沦落到吃老鼠。

80
00:42:01.000 --> 00:42:23.000
No, it's not quite the Sieger-Leningrad in the Second World War. But unless something drastically changes, Shemichel is doomed. But you know what? General Conrad is waiting in the wings for another crack. He hasn't given up. So he's going to come to the rescue. If there's one man you want to come to the rescue, it's this man with an unbroken track record of catastrophic failure.
不，这还不完全是第二次世界大战中的列宁格勒围城战。但除非发生剧烈变化，普热梅希尔（Shemichel）就注定要灭亡了。但你知道吗？康拉德将军正在一旁伺机发动另一次进攻。他还没有放弃。所以他要来救援了。如果要找一个人来救援，那就是这个有着一连串灾难性失败记录的人。

81
00:42:23.000 --> 00:43:00.000
The Gandalf the White of the Eastern Front. So his men have been holed up in the Carpathian Mountains. They've been camping in heavy snow and driving rain. They've all got frostbites and hypothermia. Another commander might say, I'll let them rest a bit and thaw out before throwing them back into the fray. But Comrade, as we know, is a born optimist. So on the 23rd of January, he says, right, I want 175,000 men to go down out the mountains, retake the mountain passes from the Russians, get down into Galicia, relieve the city. By any standards, this is a big ask, as people say.
东线的白袍甘道夫。所以他的手下一直被困在喀尔巴阡山脉中。他们一直在大雪和暴雨中露营。他们都得了冻疮和低体温症。换作别的指挥官，可能会说，在把他们重新投入战场之前，先让他们休息一会儿，暖和暖和。但正如我们所知，康拉德是个天生的乐观主义者。因此，在1月23日，他说，好吧，我要175,000人从山上下来，从俄军手中夺回山口，进入加利西亚，解救这座城市。无论按什么标准来看，正如人们所说，这都是一个艰巨的要求。

82
00:43:00.000 --> 00:43:26.000
And actually, what happens next is the Carpathian campaign. Now, again, this is one of those things that I think in the English-speaking world, most people have no idea this ever happened. I put my hand up, I plead guilty. It's one of the most horrific campaigns of the whole war. So hundreds of thousands of Austro-Hungarians are trying to fight their way out to these mountains. They're in snow blizzards. The temperatures are below minus 20 degrees Celsius.
而事实上，接下来发生的就是喀尔巴阡山战役。现在，这也是英语世界里大多数人根本不知道发生过的事情之一。我举手承认，我难辞其咎。这是整个战争中最可怕的战役之一。数十万奥匈帝国官兵试图在这些山脉中杀出一条血路。他们身处暴风雪中。气温降至零下20摄氏度以下。

83
